1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,448
The quarter is full before it starts.

2
00:00:03,448 --> 00:00:07,882
A new first-task card is due in two weeks.

3
00:00:07,882 --> 00:00:11,823
A reminder system is penciled in after that.

4
00:00:11,823 --> 00:00:17,241
Someone has promised a dashboard by the end of the month.

5
00:00:17,241 --> 00:00:28,079
The schedule looks responsible, but the team still cannot answer a basic question: why do new members hesitate at the first task?

6
00:00:28,079 --> 00:00:32,020
Our feedback ledger gave us a possible explanation.

7
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:35,961
The roadmap bet gave us a small test.

8
00:00:35,961 --> 00:00:41,379
Chapter Ten asks us to put the learning beside the delivery.

9
00:00:41,379 --> 00:00:51,724
Write the outcome we seek at the top: a member can understand and begin the first task without staff translating it.

10
00:00:51,724 --> 00:00:56,158
Under that, write what we need to learn first.

11
00:00:56,158 --> 00:01:01,084
Is the barrier unclear instruction, weak confirmation, or something else?

12
00:01:01,084 --> 00:01:06,010
A feature date can tell us when work will ship.

13
00:01:06,010 --> 00:01:15,369
A learning checkpoint tells us when we will inspect what the work taught us and decide whether to continue.

14
00:01:15,369 --> 00:01:21,773
Let us build two checkpoints for the membership service we have been following.

15
00:01:21,773 --> 00:01:26,207
We are planning the questions before any member responds.

16
00:01:26,207 --> 00:01:31,626
Keep the dates visible, but make each date earn a question.

17
00:01:31,626 --> 00:01:40,000
Otherwise a team can meet every deadline while its original uncertainty travels untouched into the next quarter.

18
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,451
Checkpoint one is a diagnosis.

19
00:01:42,451 --> 00:01:50,294
The question is whether new members understand the first instruction and the purpose of the task.

20
00:01:50,294 --> 00:01:58,627
The method could be a small observation of the starting moment, paired with the original feedback IDs.

21
00:01:58,627 --> 00:02:03,529
The owner schedules the review before a larger build begins.

22
00:02:03,529 --> 00:02:12,843
If participants can explain the action but cannot tell whether pressing begin worked, confirmation becomes the stronger next question.

23
00:02:12,843 --> 00:02:20,196
If they cannot explain what the task is for, the instruction and purpose deserve attention.

24
00:02:20,196 --> 00:02:28,529
If both are clear, we reopen the problem instead of forcing one of our planned features through.

25
00:02:28,529 --> 00:02:30,980
Write those possible adjustments now.

26
00:02:30,980 --> 00:02:36,373
This is what the book means by making learning a deliverable.

27
00:02:36,373 --> 00:02:44,216
The team is accountable for running a sound test, reviewing the evidence, and recording a decision.

28
00:02:44,216 --> 00:02:49,608
It cannot honestly guarantee the customer outcome before seeing what happens.

29
00:02:49,608 --> 00:02:53,039
Keep the method proportionate to the question.

30
00:02:53,039 --> 00:02:58,922
A team walkthrough can catch confusing copy before a participant sees it.

31
00:02:58,922 --> 00:03:02,353
It cannot tell us how members behave.

32
00:03:02,353 --> 00:03:11,667
For that, invite people through a consented research process and keep their notes where your team protects customer information.

33
00:03:11,667 --> 00:03:20,000
It is a blueprint for the decision, with enough detail to let another person run the checkpoint.

34
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Checkpoint two follows only if the first review supports the instruction hypothesis.

35
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:33,500
The team tests the smaller first-task card against the existing version in a planned study.

36
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:37,500
Before observing anyone, define what “clear enough” means.

37
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:47,500
In a moderated task, can the person identify the next action and say why it matters without a staff explanation?

38
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:51,000
What happens when the start action fails?

39
00:03:51,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Record the observation method, the number and type of participants the study can responsibly support, and the date the team will decide.

40
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:12,500
The exact threshold is a local design choice for the real test; we will not invent one and call it evidence.

41
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:39,000
On the card, write the condition in plain terms: “If most observed participants still need translation, revise the card and retest before expanding scope.” Also write the alternative: “If they understand the card but do not return, investigate the reminder question.” This is a branch, not a promise that a reminder will work.

42
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,500
The roadmap now shows a delivery task and two learning reviews.

43
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:47,500
People can still plan engineering time.

44
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:53,000
They can also see where a result may change that plan.

45
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
That is more useful than a date that only asks whether the ticket closed.

46
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:05,208
Chapter Eleven tells a story about Sam and her team.

47
00:05:05,208 --> 00:05:11,458
A junior product manager brings an unfinished idea into the team channel.

48
00:05:11,458 --> 00:05:15,625
Sam expects to step in and shape it.

49
00:05:15,625 --> 00:05:27,604
Instead, colleagues ask what customer outcome the idea addresses, whether the job has been validated, and what they would learn by testing it.

50
00:05:27,604 --> 00:05:33,854
The point of that moment is not that every conversation became perfect.

51
00:05:33,854 --> 00:05:45,833
It is that the team had a shared way to examine an idea without waiting for one leader to supply all the judgment.

52
00:05:45,833 --> 00:05:50,521
I am using Sam as the book's narrative illustration.

53
00:05:50,521 --> 00:05:55,208
That moment belongs to Sam’s story in the book.

54
00:05:55,208 --> 00:06:03,542
In the story, the team also stops treating sunk cost as a reason to keep building.

55
00:06:03,542 --> 00:06:13,958
That matters because a learning checkpoint only works if the people in the room can act on what they find.

56
00:06:13,958 --> 00:06:22,812
If the review date arrives and everyone is afraid to change the plan, the checkpoint is decoration.

57
00:06:22,812 --> 00:06:31,146
Put the owner, evidence, and possible adjustment on the roadmap where the team can see them.

58
00:06:31,146 --> 00:06:40,000
Make the question normal enough for engineering, design, support, and leadership to use in their own words.

59
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:47,614
The book gives Sam's team a test that does not support the feature they built.

60
00:06:47,614 --> 00:06:53,706
They try document previews, expecting people to find the right file faster.

61
00:06:53,706 --> 00:06:58,782
The review points them toward a different need: better search.

62
00:06:58,782 --> 00:07:02,843
The team records the learning and changes direction.

63
00:07:02,843 --> 00:07:10,964
What matters for us is how the team responded when the test challenged its original bet.

64
00:07:10,964 --> 00:07:12,995
Look at the sequence.

65
00:07:12,995 --> 00:07:15,025
They stated a hypothesis.

66
00:07:15,025 --> 00:07:18,579
They chose a signal and review point.

67
00:07:18,579 --> 00:07:20,609
They inspected the result.

68
00:07:20,609 --> 00:07:26,193
Then they asked what the result meant for the user's job.

69
00:07:26,193 --> 00:07:36,345
A low number by itself would not tell them whether the feature was hidden, unwanted, or solving the wrong task.

70
00:07:36,345 --> 00:07:38,376
The follow-up conversation mattered.

71
00:07:38,376 --> 00:07:41,929
Apply that discipline to our membership example.

72
00:07:41,929 --> 00:07:52,589
If the first-task card gets used but members still need staff help, do not declare success because the card was opened.

73
00:07:52,589 --> 00:07:59,188
If people never reach it, check the entry path before judging its wording.

74
00:07:59,188 --> 00:08:05,279
If the instruction helps but the next step fails, write that down.

75
00:08:05,279 --> 00:08:12,386
The team can hold delivery accountable and still let evidence change the next bet.

76
00:08:12,386 --> 00:08:20,000
That is a practical promise to the people who have to live with the roadmap.

77
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:27,254
Pause and choose one delivery milestone from your own plan or from our example.

78
00:08:27,254 --> 00:08:30,363
Add two learning checkpoints beside it.

79
00:08:30,363 --> 00:08:41,762
For each checkpoint, write the question, the evidence you will use, the owner, the review date, and the adjustment you could make.

80
00:08:41,762 --> 00:08:47,461
The first should reduce uncertainty before the team commits too much.

81
00:08:47,461 --> 00:08:51,606
The second should inspect the smallest useful test.

82
00:08:51,606 --> 00:09:00,933
If a result would never change the plan, rewrite the question until the review has a real consequence.

83
00:09:00,933 --> 00:09:11,295
Resume when you can tell a teammate what you will know at each date and what you may do differently.

84
00:09:11,295 --> 00:09:16,477
Use information you have permission to share on this worksheet.

85
00:09:16,477 --> 00:09:20,622
Keep participant notes in your team’s research system.

86
00:09:20,622 --> 00:09:29,948
Your decision file now has a source trail, a living product handoff, and a roadmap that can learn.

87
00:09:29,948 --> 00:09:37,720
The two checkpoint cards make the next decision visible to everyone who owns the work.

88
00:09:37,720 --> 00:09:47,047
In the next lesson, we will define the measures and write the full learning plan before results arrive.

89
00:09:47,047 --> 00:09:53,264
A plan becomes more trustworthy when it shows where it can change.

90
00:09:53,264 --> 00:10:00,000
The date is useful because someone has agreed to look, listen, and decide.
